# ── Byte-exact engine assets ────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Committed .inf_* payloads are locked byte-for-byte by tests (generator locks,
# forever-load schema fixtures, golden scenes). Line-ending conversion on
# checkout would silently break those locks on Windows (it did: Coyote.inf_act
# is JSON text, and autocrlf checkout CRLF'd it under the Windows CI runner).
# Never let git touch them.
*.inf_lvl -text
*.inf_act -text
*.inf_fn -text
*.inf_mat -text
*.inf_mati -text
*.inf_tex -text
*.inf_pcg -text
*.inf_enum -text
*.inf_struct -text
*.inf_table -text
*.inf_anim -text
*.inf_skel -text
*.inf_sm -text
# P29.6: the `.inf_sm` TEXT face (pillar S1). It is a sidecar rather than a
# payload, so nothing locks its bytes yet -- but `phase29_gate` reads it, edits
# one line of it and compares the result, and the whole point of the file is
# that a diff of it is reviewable. A checkout that CRLF'd it would make the
# committed line endings a property of the developer's platform, which is the
# exact defect the block above records paying for once already.
*.inf_sm.txt -text
# SCRIPT1b: `.infini` is InfiniScript SOURCE -- the first asset kind in this list
# whose payload is text a human wrote, which makes `-text` look wrong and is
# exactly why it is right. The LEXER normalises CRLF, a lone CR and a leading
# byte-order mark, so what a script MEANS is insensitive to all three
# (`inf-script`'s determinism gate). What cannot be normalised is the file's
# **identity**: a `.infini` with no committed sidecar gets a GUID synthesised
# from its CONTENT HASH (`AssetDb::scan`), and a hash is over bytes. So a
# checkout that CRLF'd a committed script would give it a different asset GUID
# on Windows than on Linux -- the same program under a different name, in a pack
# whose entries are keyed by that name. The fourth catch of the CRLF class, met
# from the one direction the lexer cannot cover.
*.infini -text
# .inf_biomes (P19.2): a BiomeSet is deterministic bincode whose bytes a cook
# validates and a fixture will lock; never let git rewrite them.
*.inf_biomes -text
# .ipack: the frozen v1 pack fixture (P16.1 back-compat gate) is compared
# byte-for-byte; a cooked ship pack is binary by construction.
*.ipack -text
# The remaining committed binary payloads (I3). Every one of these is locked
# byte-for-byte by `samples::tests::committed_sample_matches_generators` —
# `.inf_mesh` five times over, `.inf_terrain` and `.inf_voxel` by the P21/P22
# samples, `.inf_audio` by the playgrounds — and every one was relying on git's
# BINARY AUTO-DETECTION to survive a Windows checkout rather than on a rule.
#
# Auto-detection works (it reads NUL bytes in the first 8 KiB) right up until a
# payload's first block happens not to contain one, and this file's own header
# records what that costs: `Coyote.inf_act` was mangled exactly that way. A lock
# that depends on a heuristic is a lock with a coin flip in it. `.inf_vmesh` and
# `.inf_fracture` are derived at cook and not committed today; they are listed
# because the day one is, the rule should already be here.
*.inf_mesh -text
*.inf_vmesh -text
*.inf_terrain -text
*.inf_voxel -text
*.inf_audio -text
*.inf_fracture -text
*.png -text

# TOML (asset sidecars included) participates in byte-determinism tests and is
# always emitted with LF; check it out that way everywhere.
*.toml text eol=lf

# Rust sources are READ BY TESTS, not only compiled (P22.4).
#
# The mirror gates (`projector_mirror`, `viewport_pump_mirror`, `grammar_span_mirror`)
# and the P22.4 trig-law gate all `include_str!`/`read_to_string` a `.rs` file and
# index into it — `find("\n}\n")`, `rfind('\n')`, line walks. With `core.autocrlf =
# true` and no attribute, every one of those files is checked out CRLF on Windows,
# where `"\n}\n"` does not occur and the search returns `None`. The mirrors defend
# themselves with `.replace("\r\n", "\n")` in twenty places; relying on every future
# gate to remember is the same bet this file's first paragraph already lost once.
#
# `text eol=lf` does not change what is stored (git normalizes to LF on check-IN
# regardless) — it fixes what is written to the working tree, so a Windows checkout
# and a Linux one hand a test the same bytes.
*.rs text eol=lf

# WGSL is read by tests for the same reason (P25.2). `inf-photo-gpu`'s parity
# gate strips comment lines out of every shader in its `SHADERS` table and then
# asserts what the CODE contains — that it never calls `fma()` (which would
# guarantee the contraction the CPU reference does not perform) and never calls
# `round()` (round-half-to-even in WGSL against Rust's round-half-away-from-zero).
# `str::lines()` happens to tolerate a trailing `\r`, so today the check survives
# a CRLF checkout by luck rather than by rule. The rule is cheaper than the luck.
*.wgsl text eol=lf

# Markdown is READ BY TESTS too (P29.6). `committed_sample_matches_generators`
# byte-pins every file of `samples/phase29-locomotion`, including its README —
# the first .md whose bytes a gate compares against a generator's LF output. On
# the Windows CI runner an autocrlf checkout handed that pin CRLF and the gate
# red was a property of the platform, not the content (the block above's story,
# fourth telling). Stored blobs are already LF; this fixes what checkout writes.
*.md text eol=lf

# TypeScript is GENERATED by ts-rs, which writes LF (island phase I1; the
# certification's D-26). Without this, every Windows contributor finished a green
# battery with ~150 dirty `editor/studio/src/bindings/*.ts` files whose
# `git diff --numstat` reports **zero changed lines** — pure checkout churn, and
# a drift-check job that has to be told to ignore it. Same fix, same reason, as
# the `.rs` / `.wgsl` / `.md` blocks above: this does not change what is stored
# (git normalizes to LF on check-IN regardless), it fixes what checkout writes.
*.ts text eol=lf
*.tsx text eol=lf

# Python joins the list at wave ASSET0 with `tools/ue-export/export.py`, the
# first .py this repository has held. Indentation IS the syntax, the harness
# that edits these files has already eaten a line continuation once (the P22
# law), and a script whose line endings are a property of whose machine
# checked it out is one more thing to rule out when it misbehaves inside
# another engine's embedded interpreter. Same fix, same reason.
*.py text eol=lf

# GeoJSON joins the list at wave ROAD1b. The island's layers are GENERATED —
# `inf_island::layers::write_*` writes them with `\n` — and one of them,
# `layers/streets.geojson`, is now byte-locked by
# `samples::tests::committed_sample_matches_generators`, which compares the file
# on disk against a fresh generation. A checkout that CRLF'd it turns that lock
# red on Windows and green on Linux, which is the fifth telling of the same law.
# Measured, not anticipated: stashing the freshly written layer and popping it
# put 8 268 carriage returns into it.
*.geojson text eol=lf

# PowerShell and ESM join the list at the wave CHAR1a audit, and the trigger was
# a commit warning rather than a theory: with `core.autocrlf = true` — which is
# this machine's setting — `git commit` printed *"in the working copy of
# 'tools/demo/demo.ps1', LF will be replaced by CRLF the next time Git touches
# it"* for both `tools/demo/demo.ps1` and `tools/demo/place.mjs`, because neither
# extension had a rule and neither is `text=auto`-declared. They are LF today
# only because they were written locally. `place.mjs` is a **new** file, so the
# next fresh Windows clone would have been the first to see CRLF in it, and the
# CRLF law has already been caught four times in this repository by exactly that
# route. Same fix, same reason as the `.py` block above.
*.ps1 text eol=lf
*.mjs text eol=lf
*.cmd text eol=lf
